Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 12:38:03 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

Hal Finney writes:
>First, let me offer a definition of zero-sum since Robin wasn't able
>to get one from anybody else :-).  A series of transactions is
>zero-sum (positive-sum, negative-sum) if the market value of the goods
>and cash involved in the transactions, summed over all the
>participants, is the same as (greater than, less than) it was when
>they started. ...
>If Robin and I bet each other every week on the outcome of a football
>game, or which horse will win a race, we won't jointly make any money
>on these transactions.  These are zero-sum activities, and they seem
>very similar to the futures transaction example I gave. 

Nonsense.  As I said to Perry on 26 Jan 93:

>Imagine I bet you my treasury bond to your treasury bond that my team
>will win the Superbowl.  Our total average return, using a common
>probability of winning for calculations, is just the return of the
>treasury bond, minus transaction costs.  This can be positive.

It is trivial to have bets where the sum of values at time t1 is less
than the sum of the values at t2, and the bet "happened" between t1
and t2.  Imagine that we bet shares in my index mutual fund of the
S&P500.  Then the rate of return on our betting assets is exactly the
same as the S&P500 (ignoring transcation costs).

Perhaps you need to be even more precise in your definition.  Give an
equation in terms of the market prices of each asset, who owns them,
and at at what times you mean to evaluate those market prices.

SIDE EDITORIAL:  Libertarians tend to thumb their noses at all those
mathematical economists, endlessly building irrelevant models when the
basic truths are easy to understand intuitively.  But this example
shows the value of math; when intuitions clash, math can reveal who's
being sloppy in their thinking.  I am coming to more respect econ math.

Robin Hanson

