Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 12:31:24 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

> From: KMOSTA01@ulkyvx02.louisville.edu
> 
> I communicated with Robin privately until I understood what he meant
> and now will support him, but would like to ask you all to consider the
> fact that Robin, in my opinion, more or less wants you all to realize
> the economic (not just accounting) side of futures -- the speculators
> add value by assuming the risk. I just can't figure out why Robin
> insists on calling it "betting" bundling it with Las Vegas casinos,
> where risk is artificially created, while the commodities futures
> (and stocks market) risk will exist whether those markets exist or not.

I've been saying the whole time that insurance and futures markets ARE
of great economic value. I've merely been noting that they are zero
sum games and that casual investors should realize that only half the
transactions engaged in will make money. The mere fact that I lose money
on average from my car insurance plan doesn't mean that car insurance
isn't of net economic benefit to me, however, and the mere fact that half
the investments in futures markets must by definition lose money doesn't
mean that futures aren't of benefit.

However, I dispair of the notion that anyone is going to get my point,
which is fairly subtle.

This whole thing started because someone was trying to get people to join
him in doing some futures investing, which I noted is generally suicidal.
The "zero sum" nature of the beast is why, but it doesn't seem anyone
has picked up on this point, and I guess I'll quit at it now.

