Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 15:21:50 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: The rights of the infected

> From: lubkin@apollo.hp.com
> 
> In a libertarian society, what restrictions/rights should
> people with infectious diseases have?  When does their
> presence in a location or activity in some profession
> constitute an infringement of the legitimate rights of
> someone else?

There are lots of such "hard problems". I can name a few more -- do you
have the right to build a building that blocks someone's view?
You can shine a flashlight at your neighbor's house, but what about a
10 megawatt laser? You have some right to play your stereo in your home --
but at what point of volume does it become a public problem?

These questions, in general, have no one right answer. The way that
they have been solved traditionally is by precedent. So long as the courts
maintain a consistant standard and everyone understands what is expected
of them, everything will be fine.

Myself, were I asked to rule I'd say that someone knowingly exposing
others to harm, whether by generating dangerous bacteria from their
own bodies or placing an incubator on their desk spraying those bacteria
into the air, is legally liable for the damage those organisms do. However,
other standards might be possible.

Perry


