> |> 
> |> Iceland had land ownership.[Snide "old-Perry style" comment deleted]
> True, but --
> 
> If the question revolves around how to do something within a
> simply-connected voluntarist community, then the Icelandic experience
> may be a good guide; but they never had more than one legal code at a
> time, so any question of what institutions might evolve in a world
> with a non-trivial free market in law can't be solved by appeal to
> Iceland.

I really don't think we WILL have more than one legal code at a time in
practice. Transaction costs and other pressures will, in my opinion, result
in the standardization of the bulk of the law. Trivialities will differ,
but I suspect in the long run we will end up with contracts appealing
in a clause to "This contract is held to be governed by the rules published
in "West's Standard Commercial Code"", or something similar.

People thinking in terms of large numbers of competing legal systems are
not, I think, operating within the proper paradigm. There are some items
that end up operating in the market in a competitive mode -- take, for
example, the sale of paper. There are other things in the market that
standardize of their own accord -- VCR tape formats, or the 8.5x11 inch
paper standard.

Certainly in a PPL environment different proprietary communities and the like
will have different ideas about whether you can make noise after 11pm,
or whether its O.K. to build your house out of dynamite sticks. On the
other hand, its extremely unlikely in my opinion that fundamental questions
about property rights, legal compensation and contracts are going to
remain up in the air. Land ownership, contract law, parliamentary proceedure,
and the like are quite likely to standardize, and very fast. This is not
to say that they are likely to standardize around a legislated legal
code -- this is just to say that market pressures will likely produce
a standardized customary legal code for these matters because commerce is
so widespread that without them it would be difficult to conduct large
scale trade.

I suspect that Benson's notion of "customary law" conveys the flavor of
the paradigm better than the notion of "Privately Produced Law", which
lends a notion of competing governments, which is not what has been
seen in practice in the past ans which is not likely to show up
in the future. 

> One could make a gedanken experiment of Joe's Protection Racket, which
> covers land claims, vs. Chief Seattle's Ecotopia, which doesn't, and
> show that Joe's clients come out ahead if land has value in excess of
> the cost of protecting it ...

Beyond the mere economic pressure, which will likely be extensive, we
get the pressure associated with "societal compatibility", which may
be thought of in much the same way as the notion of VCR compatibility.
There is a good example in that new book who's name I sadly forget, which
used as its extended example the question of open vs. closed range lands
in a western state. In one flavor of law, landowners are expected to fence
their land in and are responsible for the incursion of other people's
cattle. In another flavor, it is the owners of the cattle that are held
responsible for their incursions. One state has a patchwork of both kinds
of laws, but somehow, everyone ignores them and by custom its generally held
that the cattle owner is always responsible for his own cattle.

Similarly, I suspect that people with no respect for land rights and people
with respect for them would not be able to coexist for long -- the tensions
associated with continuous court payments and transfers would eventually
lead to one side giving up -- and I'd suspect it would be the "no land title"
folks.

Prehaps this should be discussed more extensively.

Perry


