   I'm not sure what you mean here.  The point of malpractice insurance
   is to reduce your worst-case exposure.  And everybody has malpractice
   insurance, for the reason you state.

But insurance policies always have upper limits, and given juries
these days, there's no way to know for certain that you won't get hit
by an award greater than your limit.  Some awards are truly insane
(Ford got hit by a $128 million award, but it was reduced to $5
million or so, although that wasn't a medical malpractice case.), but
even sane awards can be incredibly high (In the Ford case, the
defendant had $3 million in medical expenses.).  So, in practice, it's
not possible to really protect yourself on the high end, if there's no
limit to what the award might be.

   > On the other hand, having the ob/gyn's insurance pay the lifetime
   > costs of a child who is congenitally damaged is actually a rather
   > market-friendly idea!

   Except that they can't pass the costs on because they don't set
   their fees.

True, and fee-controls have gotten stricter in the last few years, so
I tend to forget about them.  But I wouldn't be surprised if the fee
fixers will generally allow insurance premium increases to be passed
through.

   > Thus, the parents are bearing the averaged cost of
   > congenital problems in the babies they generate...

   It would make more sense to do this averaging over classes of
   parents rather than classes of ob/gyns.  That is, why not have parents
   buy such insurance if they so choose?

True.  But I'm not arguing that the present system is ideal, but that
it does *approximate* a market mechanism, rather than just being
"juries gone wild", which is what I thought it was at first.

